# Beads Issue Tracking

This project uses [beads_rust (br)](https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/beads_rust) for issue tracking.

`br` is non-invasive and never runs git commands. The local database is
`.beads/beads.db`; the portable, git-tracked representation is `.beads/issues.jsonl`.
Issue ids use the `t3-` prefix (epic children: `t3-<epic>.<n>`).

## Core Rules

- Track ALL work in `br` (never use markdown TODOs or comment-based task lists)
- Use `br ready` to find available work
- Use `br create` to track new issues/tasks/bugs
- Read AGENTS.md for model attribution, file-reservation and handoff rules
- Run `br --help` or `br <command> --help` for the installed command reference
- Default to conservative git authority: report status and proposed commands unless the user, orchestrator, or repository profile explicitly authorizes commit/sync/push

## Quick Reference

```bash
br ready                              # Show issues ready to work (no blockers)
br list --status=open                 # List all open issues
br create "title" -t task -p 2        # Create new issue
br update <id> --claim --actor "<agent>" --model "<model>" --append-notes "worked: <agent> started"
br update <id> --status open --assignee "" --model "<model>" --append-notes "worked: <agent> stopped — <reason>; left: <remaining work>"
br close <id> --model "<model>" --reason "Completed; models=<chain>"
br dep add <issue> <depends-on>       # Add dependency
br sync --flush-only                  # Export local issue changes
# When committing is authorized:
git add .beads/
git commit -m "sync beads"
```

## Workflow

1. Check for ready work: `br ready`
2. Claim an issue atomically and append attribution as shown above
3. Reserve the files you will edit via Agent Mail (reason = bead id)
4. Do the work
5. Append a completed contributor note and close with `br close <id>`
6. Handoff: report changed files, validation, issue status, and any proposed commit/sync/push commands

## Issue Types

- `bug` - Something broken
- `feature` - New functionality
- `task` - Work item (tests, docs, refactoring)
- `epic` - Large feature with subtasks
- `chore` - Maintenance (dependencies, tooling)

## Priorities

- `0` - Critical (security, data loss, broken builds)
- `1` - High (major features, important bugs)
- `2` - Medium (default, nice-to-have)
- `3` - Low (polish, optimization)
- `4` - Backlog (future ideas)

## Context Loading

Read AGENTS.md when context is missing.
For detailed commands, run `br --help` or see
[beads_rust](https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/beads_rust).

